WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:07.070
As long as people have been able to communicate, they have used codes to pass secret information to their friends and allies.

2
00:00:07.071 --> 00:00:14.469
From the battlefields of ancient Sparta to the modern Internet, codes and ciphers have proved essential when security is at stake.

3
00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:25.347
Cryptography—from the Greek words kryptos , meaning "hidden," and graphos , meaning "writing"—is the art of converting a message from its original form into one that hides

4
00:00:25.348 --> 00:00:32.712
its meaning. The encrypted message is supposed to be understood only by its author and its intended recipient, who knows how to

5
00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:40.658
read it. Humans have found ingenious ways not only to create and transmit secret communications, but to intercept and decipher them

6
00:00:40.659 --> 00:00:46.488
as well. In order to communicate secretly, people have tried to conceal the very existence of

7
00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:53.368
their messages. For example, the ancient Greeks commonly used wax-covered wooden tablets to write on, scratching words into

8
00:00:53.369 --> 00:00:57.697
the wax. They would reuse them by simply melting new layers of wax onto the

9
00:00:57.698 --> 00:01:05.151
tablets. To keep something secret, they would write a message on the bare wood tablet and only then cover it with wax to make it look

10
00:01:05.152 --> 00:01:11.402
unused. When the tablet reached its destination, the recipient would melt the wax off to see the information hidden

11
00:01:11.403 --> 00:01:20.238
underneath. A Greek general relayed a message to an ally using a different technique: he shaved the head of a trusted slave and tattooed the information onto his

12
00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:25.887
head. After his hair grew out, the message was completely hidden, and the slave was sent to deliver

13
00:01:25.888 --> 00:01:36.722
it. Messages sent through methods like these, however, were easily intercepted if someone found out about their presence, so other ways to communicate in secrecy were

14
00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:41.935
developed. One of the first documented uses of a cipher was by Hebrew scribes in the ancient Middle

15
00:01:41.936 --> 00:01:54.375
East. As early as 600 BC, these scribes encrypted parts of their texts by using the "Atbash cipher," in which they replaced the first letter of the alphabet with the last letter, the second with the second to last, and so

16
00:01:54.376 --> 00:01:59.533
on. In the English version of the Atbash cipher, the letter a would instead appear as

17
00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:07.683
z . This simple process made text unreadable to anyone who did not have the key—in this case, the fact that the alphabet

18
00:02:07.684 --> 00:02:12.920
was reversed. By 500 BC, the Spartans of Greece were using cryptography on

19
00:02:12.921 --> 00:02:19.180
the battlefield. They passed messages with a scytale, a thin strip of leather wound tightly around a

20
00:02:19.181 --> 00:02:25.130
wooden stick. The secret message was written across the leather after it was wrapped around the stick, from one end of the stick to

21
00:02:25.131 --> 00:02:32.909
the other. The leather strip was then unwound, removed from the wood, and carried to its destination—as something as innocent as a belt, for

22
00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:37.342
instance. The recipient of the strip would then wind it around another stick of identical

23
00:02:37.343 --> 00:02:46.813
size. The letters that formed the message would line up once again, and the message could easily be read; if the stick was not the right size, the text would be

24
00:02:46.814 --> 00:02:54.655
incomprehensible. Several hundred years later, Julius Caesar also used a cipher during his many military campaigns to keep his messages

25
00:02:54.656 --> 00:03:00.208
secret. For each letter of the message, he substituted the one that appears three letters later in the

26
00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:04.753
alphabet. So the letter a would be replaced with d, b with e , and

27
00:03:04.754 --> 00:03:10.683
so on. Although the Caesar cipher is simple by today’s standards, it gave Caesar a great

28
00:03:10.684 --> 00:03:14.635
strategic advantage. The Middle Ages and Renaissance saw a rapid rise

29
00:03:14.636 --> 00:03:22.392
in cryptography. The ability to communicate secretly became a priority not only of military commanders, but also of kings

30
00:03:22.393 --> 00:03:34.730
and bishops. In the fifteenth century, Leon Battista Alberti, an Italian architect, made a breakthrough in cryptography when he placed together two metal disks—one smaller, one larger—with the alphabet printed

31
00:03:34.731 --> 00:03:41.021
on each. The letters of the alphabet on the outer disk were in the correct order, while the letters on the inner circle were

32
00:03:41.022 --> 00:03:52.288
mixed up. A key indicated how to line up the disks—for instance, the message sender and its recipient would agree to rotate the inner disk to match its letter c with the letter a on the

33
00:03:52.289 --> 00:04:00.932
outer disk. Then, based on the pairings of the letters with the disks in that position, the message could easily be converted into the cipher and

34
00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:09.264
back again. Although many mechanical devices were designed for use by cryptographers, few major advances were made in cryptography until the

35
00:04:09.265 --> 00:04:19.427
nineteenth century. During the 1830s, Samuel Morse invented a new telegraph and Morse code, a system to encode letters into combinations of dots and

36
00:04:19.428 --> 00:04:25.638
dashes. This allowed messages to be tapped out and transmitted over wires in the form of short and long

37
00:04:25.639 --> 00:04:31.581
tones. By the beginning of the twentieth century, cryptography was routinely used by governments around the

38
00:04:31.582 --> 00:04:43.342
world. The telegraph machine had given way to radio, and the battle to create new methods of encryption intensified, because for every code invented, people known as "code breakers" worked tirelessly to solve the

39
00:04:43.343 --> 00:04:50.832
riddle. Governments created special departments to create new ciphers, as well as to intercept and decipher others' secret

40
00:04:50.833 --> 00:04:57.499
communications. As World War I loomed in Europe, the ability of nations to send and receive encrypted messages freely was

41
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:07.843
crucial. Portable "cipher machines" first came into use at this time; they enabled military commanders to communicate with their headquarters quickly and securely, even from remote

42
00:05:07.844 --> 00:05:12.586
locations. Amid the chaos of the battlefield, the cipher machines had to be protected at all

43
00:05:12.587 --> 00:05:20.045
costs. If they fell into enemy hands, information might be revealed that would forever alter the course of events, as history has

44
00:05:20.046 --> 00:05:25.813
shown. During World War II, the ability to decipher encrypted communications proved

45
00:05:25.814 --> 00:05:33.068
instrumental. The United States won the critical Battle of Midway against the Japanese after breaking the code used by Japan's

46
00:05:33.069 --> 00:05:42.188
military. England was able to prepare for and withstand the Germans' air and submarine attacks because the British had broken the ciphers created by the German Enigma

47
00:05:42.189 --> 00:05:52.610
machine. The Enigma was so important that, in order to hide the fact it had been broken, England resorted to other explanations for its ability to resist the Germans'

48
00:05:52.611 --> 00:05:57.896
assaults. Since World War II, the science of encryption has taken a giant leap

49
00:05:57.897 --> 00:06:03.168
forward. The introduction of computers has made codes and ciphers both easier to create and to

50
00:06:03.169 --> 00:06:08.168
break. As a result, a whole industry has risen to provide for people's security needs.

51
00:06:08.169 --> 00:06:14.104
Today, cryptography is a common part of most people's daily lives, whether they know it or not.

52
00:06:14.105 --> 00:06:23.591
Whenever people enter a password on the Internet or punch a PIN (personal identification number) into an ATM, they are using a secret code.

53
00:06:23.592 --> 00:06:30.310
Every email message is encrypted after it leaves the computer and deciphered when it arrives at its destination.

54
00:06:30.311 --> 00:06:39.353
Hackers—people who try to break into the computer systems of governments or corporations—are actually attempting to break the codes used to write software.

55
00:06:39.354 --> 00:06:48.005
The desire for secrecy has been a part of human nature for thousands of years, and as humans have evolved, so has the art of concealing information.

56
00:06:48.006 --> 00:06:59.194
As electronic communication becomes an even greater part of our lives, new, tougher codes will be developed, and the importance of cryptography will continue to increase for future generations.

